title: 'C-c s R : skg-set-relRepo-recursive'
pid: 360b816c-e688-48f2-8b02-7a8448019f13
body: |2-
  Like 'skg-set-relRepo', but applied throughout the
  subtree at point (the node at point's own relationship to its viewparent
  included). Also reachable as =C-u C-c s r=.

  It first presents an org-menu asking what kinds of relations to
  the viewparent should qualify: the schema's four node-node
  relations are the level-1 headlines, and each level-2 headline is a
  role the view-child would play in that relation toward its
  viewparent. Only two roles are settable from the child's
  position -- =content= (ordinary content) and =forkee= (a
  claiming forksFolder member); RET on any other role explains why its
  relationship cannot be set from there (write-protected folders belong
  to the other end; links carry no independent skgrepo; hide skgrepos
  are derived at save). RET picks a
  role; q aborts.

  It then prompts for a skgrepo over every skgrepo, plus the
  no-override choice, which instead removes existing skgrepo requests
  throughout the subtree. Unlike the single-relationship command, no
  per-relationship default is fetched -- the subtree's relationships have different
  defaults -- so every skgrepo is offered and the save's floor check
  validates each relationship (an offered skgrepo more public than some relationship's
  default makes the save fail, naming that relationship).

  The walk applies to, and recurses on, only viewchildren that are
  members: member occurrences, through the two
  editable folders. It sets an unknown viewnode or a restricted viewnode
  too, where it stands as such a member. It prunes below write-protected nodes and
  forkees as such (their viewchildren's relationships are not
  collected at save), and prunes write-protected folders, ghosts and
  other non-vognodes entirely. It refuses, writing nothing, where a
  headline it would set holds a delete, merge or flag request ("Cannot
  request a relRepo where a delete, merge or flag request is
  pending").

  This is the gesture for republicizing a subtree's
  relationships after 'skg-set-repo-recursive' moved its nodes to a more
  public skgrepo: the sticky rule keeps each relationship at its old, more
  private skgrepo, so the contains relationships stay behind until explicitly
  moved to the new default.

  The command only edits metadata in the current buffer.
  Save the view afterward to apply the changes.
